    "question": {
      ":question/id": "7f7f50dd-df12-54e7-9f7a-bd548e8344c0",
      ":question/problem": "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y-z &=3 \\\\ y+z &=4 \\\\ z &=3\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The third equation states that $z=3.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+(3)=4.\n$$\n\n$$\ny=4-(3)=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $y=1$ and $z=3$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx-(1)-(3)=3.\n$$\n\n$$\nx=3-(-1)-(-3)=7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=7$, $y=1$, and $z=3.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
