    "question": {
      ":question/id": "e362bfec-4888-5154-a63d-9a862b204c00",
      ":question/problem": "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=\\frac{14}{3} \\\\ {x}_{2}-6{x}_{3} &=10 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=- \\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The third equation states that ${x}_{3}=- \\frac{4}{3}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-6(- \\frac{4}{3})=10.\n$$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=10-(8)=2.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{2}=2$ and ${x}_{3}=- \\frac{4}{3}$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+(2)-(- \\frac{4}{3})=\\frac{14}{3}.\n$$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{14}{3}-(2)-(\\frac{4}{3})=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{4}{3}$, ${x}_{2}=2$, and ${x}_{3}=- \\frac{4}{3}.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 2 \\\\ - \\frac{4}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$",
