      ":question/id": "588c5f6b-2c8c-5bf4-a340-4f2d0bd9b86d",
      ":question/problem": "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=1 \\\\ {x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=- \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The third equation states that ${x}_{3}=1.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-(1)=- \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=- \\frac{1}{2}-(-1)=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${x}_{3}=1$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+(\\frac{1}{2})-(1)=1.\n$$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=1-(\\frac{1}{2})-(-1)=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2}$, ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}$, and ${x}_{3}=1.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n⟨\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2},1⟩.\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
